<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.MirrorView</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>harness-mirror-view</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THIS BUILD READS HOLDING REGISTERS AND SERVES A PAGE. IT CANNOT WRITE ANYTHING.

    It points at a physical PLC, so read-only is a property of the ASSEMBLY, not an intention. The one
    wire verb reachable from here is Harness.MirrorRead.IRegisterSource.Read — an interface that carries
    no write and never hands its transport out — and the claim is CHECKED: MirrorViewStructureTests walks
    every method body in the compiled assembly and asserts none of them names a write member of
    Harness.Wire, Harness.Map or NModbus, with a denominator (bodies examined > 0) and a LIVE positive
    control (a planted class in the test assembly that really does call WriteHoldingRegisters, which the
    same walk must find). A const bool with an Assert.False on it is what this replaces: measured
    2026-08-14 on Harness.RigWrite, adding a class that constructed a live socket client left all 202
    tests green.

    The same walk asserts IPAddress.Any / IPAddress.IPv6Any appear NOWHERE, with its own planted control
    — a view of a live controller must not be reachable from a network interface.

    NO new transport code. Harness.MirrorRead already owns the read-only handle and Harness.Wire owns the
    socket, the FC03 bounds checks and ModbusPolicy (Retries = 0, which is load-bearing and is not the
    library default). A second client here would be an uncalibrated one.

    Web SDK, so Kestrel comes from the in-box Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared framework: no package
    restore, no CDN, nothing to install. The page is one static string with its CSS and JS inline.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.MirrorRead\Harness.MirrorRead.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Wire\Harness.Wire.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\device-guard\DeviceGuard\DeviceGuard.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
